plot_forward_model (data, P, plotFlag) function

This function generates a model prediction and optionally plots it on the same plots as the measurements when run with data base plot.

The prediction of cosmogenic nuclide concentration are an integral of P(z(t)) from some time of ice emplacement data.T to present and are as followed examples

Stable nuclide
N21 = integral(@(t) P_of_z( z_of_t( t ), 0, data.T)

Unstable nuclide example
N10 = integral(@(t) P_of_z( z_of_t( t ) * exp(-l10.*t), 0, data.T)

This takes a current depth of a sample, predict what its depth used to be going back in time based on z_of_t.m then find the associated production rate at those depth and integrate those through time and produce the total amount of nuclides that has accumulated during this time period under a certain environmental conditions.

Input
	Data – data structure generated from sublimation_model_params.m
	Data.N10inh – include cosmogenic nuclide inheritance (10, 21 and 26)
	P – the production rate structure
plotFlag – if omitted, plots predicted nuclide concentration. PlotFlag = 0 suppresses the plot and only provide the predicted nuclide concentration values

Output
	z – Mass depth (g cm-2)
	N10 – predicted 10Be concentrations at mass depths
N21 – predicted 21Ne concentrations at mass depths
N26 – predicted 26Al concentrations at mass depths

Additional modifications:
	plot_forward_model_zs.m
	Allows you to determine at which depth integration should be predicted for.

	plot_forward_model_sample_depth.m
	Creates model predictions based on given sample data depths.

 
% Set parameters
s_m_Myr = 20; % 1. Sublimation rate m/Myr
ET_m_Myr = 0.9; % 2. Till erosion rate
data.T = 1e6; % 3. Ice emplacement age
data.N10inh = 1e5;
data.N26inh = 7e5;
data.N21inh = 10e6;
 
% convert s to cm/yr
data.s = s_m_Myr .* 1e-4;
 
% Convert ET to g/cm2/yr
data.ET = ET_m_Myr.*100.*(110.15./62)./1e6;
% Enter measured till thickness (g/cm2)
data.Ztill = 110.15;
 
% Get implied debris densities, etc. from sublimation_model_params.m
 
data = sublimation_model_params(data);

% Load Precalculated productions rates at depth

load Pall

Ncalc = plot_forward_model(data,P,0);

 
Precalculate_P_of_z.m script

This script defines a series of depths between zero and some large values, and computes spallation, muon and total production rates at all depths. The production rates are stored as a structure of 4 vectors called P that containing the depth values and the production rates for each of the nuclides, 10Be, 26Al, and 21Ne, and saves it as a .mat file called Pall. This script has no input or arguments but does contain parameters such as elevation and latitude specific to Ong Valley drill site. If used for other locations, these should be changed accordingly.

The total production rate is the production of cosmogenic nuclide produced by spallation and muon interactions. Currently, the production due to spallation is scaled used the ‘LDSn’ method. To use the ‘St’ scaling method (stone2000.m), one would manually change the scaling equation used. 

Output (structure)
	P.zg – logarithmic matrix of mass depths (g/cm2)
	P.P – Matrix of 3 of production rates at corresponding zg depths for 10Be, 26Al, and 21Ne.

Example

precalculate_P_of_z

or

load Pall;

 
save_data_x script

These scripts save all data information for a given sample into a sampledata structure file.

The data used is obtained from various spreadsheet; include debris and ice core weights and densities, measured cosmogenic nuclide concentrations and uncertainties.

Basically, one pastes data from the various spreadsheets into this script and runs it. That will store the data in a form that can be easily used for plotting and model-data comparison later. 

Note this script includes shielding depth calculations for till and ice inside the script. That should make it easier to change these here instead of having to go back to the spreadsheet.

Saved sampledata structure:

td	Top depth of sample segment (cm)
bd	Bottom depth of sample segment (cm)
sample_name	Name of the sample segment
tdz	Top mass depth of sample segment (g/cm2)
bdz	Bottom mass depth of sample segment (g/cm2)
Mq	Mass of quartz in subsample mixed to make complete sample
Mqtd	Top depth of subsample (cm)
Mqbd	Bottom depth of subsample (cm)
Mqtdz	Top mass depth of subsample (g/cm2)
Mqbdz	Bottom mass depth of subsample (g/cm2)
allN21s	All 21Ne concentration measurements (atom/g qt)
alldN21s	All 21Ne concentration uncertainty (atom/g qt)
N21	Calculated measured average 21Ne concentration (atom/g qt)
dN21	Calculated 21Ne concentration uncertainty (atoms/g qt)
N26	Measured 26Al concentration (atoms/g qt)
dN26	Measured 26Al uncertainty (atoms/g qt)
N10	Measured 10Be concentration (atoms/g qt)
dN10	Measured 10Be uncertainty (atoms/g qt)

Note: Missing sample data information is entered as NaN and can at later time be added.
 
Stone2000 (lat, P, Fsp)

This function calculates the geographic scaling factor for cosmogenic nuclide production with varying latitude and altitude

Reference

Stone, J., 2000, Air Pressure and Cosmogenic Isotope Production. JGR 105:B10, p. 23753.


Input
	Lat – The site latitude (decimal degrees)
	p – atmospheric pressure at the site (hPa)
Fsp – fraction of production due to neutron spallation. If omitted, it defaults to 0.978 which is the correct value for 10Be. For Fsp = 1, the scaling factor is for spallation only, and Fsp = 0 the scaling factor is for production by muons only

Output
	Scaling factor – scaling factor for the surface production rate for 10Be.

Example
elv_m = 1686; % meters. 
 
pressure = antatm(elv_m); % convert to atmospheric pressure in hPa
 
sf_St = stone2000(-83.25,pressure,1); 

 
sublimation_model_params (in) function

This function calculates various parameters needed for a sublimation-erosion model for till-covered ice and adds the parameters to the input data structure. The parameters added are initial thickness of the ice, which has been consumed during model, the density of debris in mixture, the implied sediment concentration of consumed ice based on input data and the density of ice mixture.

Input
in.T - age of ice (yr)
in.s - sublimation rate, i.e. rate that ice mass surface is lowering (cm/yr)
in.ET - surface erosion rate of till (g/cm2/yr)
in.Ztill - present till thickness (g/cm2)

Output
out.z_ice_init - implied ice thickness consumed during model time
out.CDrhoM - implied density of debris in ice/debris mixture
out.CD - implied weight fraction of debris in ice
out.rhoM - implied density of ice/debris mixture

Example for using the function with the conversion into correctly used units, based on Ong Valley ice core model.
% Set parameters
s_m_Myr = 20.7; % 1. Sublimation rate m/Myr
ET_m_Myr = 0.15; % 2. Till erosion rate
data.T = 3e6; % 3. Ice emplacement age

% convert s to cm/yr
data.s = s_m_Myr .* 1e-4;
 
% Convert ET to g/cm2/yr
data.ET = ET_m_Myr.*100.*(110.15./62)./1e6;
% Enter measured till thickness (g/cm2)
data.Ztill = 110.15;
 
% Get implied debris densities, etc. from sublimation_model_params.m 
data = sublimation_model_params(data);

 
z_of_t (t,z_now,data) function

This function calculates a samples depth at some time in the past, based on an eroding-till-over-sublimating-ice model. This function works for a one dimensional matrix of t and returns an equal matrix of z

Input
	t – the time before present you want to know z at (yr)
	z_now – the present mass depth of a sample below the surface (g/cm2)
data – data structure of model parameters, e.g. age, rates, etc. and is generated by sublimation_model_params.m

Output
	z – the mass depth of the sample at time t before present

Example for using this function with visual plot
% Test z(t) for one representative sample. 

% Define sample depth and time mesh, and compute z(t) 
z = 50; % depth below surface (cm)
 
% convert sample depth to mass depth
z_now = z.*rho_till; % g/cm2
 
ts = linspace(0,data.T,100); %generate a 1x100 matrix from 0 to data.T
zs = z_of_t(ts,z_now,data);
 
figure; 
plot(ts./1e6,zs);
xlabel('Time ago (Myr)');
set(gca,'xlim',[0 data.T./1e6]);
ylabel('Depth (g/cm2)');
set(gca,'ylim',[0 (max(zs)+200)],'YDir','reverse');
 
 




